Found by actually following the install instructions inside a fresh git-controlled project and running `git add -A`, not by reading the README and assuming it was fine -- the original one-line `git clone` instruction had been sitting unquestioned since the repo's first commit.

`git clone <repo> .claude/skills/git-why` leaves a nested `.git` at that path. Since the target project is a git repo in the overwhelmingly common case, `git add` there doesn't fail or warn loudly -- it prints an advisory ("adding embedded git repository") and proceeds, staging a gitlink (mode 160000, a submodule-style pointer) instead of the actual files. Verified directly: `git ls-files -s .claude/skills/git-why` showed a single 160000 entry, not SKILL.md/scripts/etc. Nothing about the skill is actually committed. The next person to clone the target project -- a teammate, CI, a fresh machine -- gets an empty directory where the skill should be, silently, with nothing pointing at the cause.

This is a worse failure mode than the nested-directory mistake (`.claude/skills/git-why/git-why/SKILL.md`) already documented right below it in the same section: that one fails immediately and visibly (skill doesn't load, you notice on the next Claude Code session). This one fails on a *different machine*, possibly much later, for someone who never ran the install command and has no reason to suspect a stray gitlink.

Fix is one line: `rm -rf .claude/skills/git-why/.git` right after the clone, turning it into plain vendored files. Verified the fix directly too -- re-ran the same clone-then-git-add sequence with the rm -rf step added, and `git ls-files -s` showed real 100644/100755 blobs for every file instead of the gitlink.
